Tokenizer Lib:
	Llama 3 uses a byte-pair encoding algorithm built on top of OpenAI's tiktoken engine, hence tiktoken can be used as way to tokenize.

	byte-pair encoding algorithm [BPE]:
		1. The Core Mechanic: Data Compression for Text
			Byte-Pair Encoding was not originally invented for AI. It was invented in 1994 as a simple lossless data compression 
			algorithm.
			• How it works: It looks at a massive dataset of raw text, finds the most frequently repeating pairs of bytes 
					(characters), and merges them into a single new byte.
			• The Loop: It repeats this merging process over and over again for millions of cycles until it has built up a 
				    massive dictionary of highly optimized compressed text fragments.

		2. Why AI Uses It: The "Vocabulary Goldilocks Zone"
			Before BPE, older AI language systems tried to use whole words as their vocabulary base. This caused a massive 
			structural engineering crisis:
			• If you index only whole words, your dictionary size explodes to millions of entries to catch every possible 
			  variation (e.g., "run", "runs", "running", "runner"). This eats up massive amounts of GPU RAM.
			• If a user types a minor typo (like "runnning"), the model instantly throws an Out Of Vocabulary (OOV) error 
			  and crashes because the exact word string is missing from its dictionary.

		The BPE Savior: BPE sits perfectly in the middle. It naturally compresses highly frequent words into a single token 
		(like "the" or "company"), while breaking down rarer, long words into hyper-efficient subword chunks (like your "run" 
		+ "ning" realization). It makes an Out of Vocabulary error completely impossible because even if a user types a bizarre 
		gibberish typo, BPE can always fall all the way back to encoding it character-by-character using individual base bytes.
		
		3. The Llama 3 Breakthrough: Scaling the Bit-Width
		   When Meta trained Llama 3, they made a massive architectural shift in their BPE tokenizer compared to Llama 1 and 2:
			• Llama 1 & 2 Tokenizer: Had a vocabulary size of 32,000 tokens.
			• Llama 3 Tokenizer: Switched to an advanced, highly dense 128,256 token vocabulary set built natively on top 
			                     of the cl100k_base bit-profile.

	Various encoding support by tokenizer:
		The Tiktoken Encodings Family Tree:
		Encoding Name |	Vocabulary Size	  |	Primary Models Used In	      |		Key Characteristics
		--------------|-------------------|-----------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------
		o200k_base	~200,000 tokens		GPT-4o, GPT-4o-mini		The Modern Giant. Massive vocabulary optimized 
											heavily for non-English languages and code efficiency.
		--------------|-------------------|-----------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------
		cl100k_base	~100,000 tokens		GPT-4, GPT-3.5-Turbo,		The Standard. The exact engine behind Llama 3's
							text-embedding-3-small/large	tokenizer style, offering balanced compression.
		--------------|-------------------|-----------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------
		p50k_base	~50,000 tokens		Codex models, text-davinci-002 	The Code Specialist. Tailored for raw programming
							/ 003				text and legacy text models.
		--------------|-------------------|-----------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------
		r50k_base	~50,257 tokens		Original GPT-2, GPT-3 legacy	The Pioneer. The ancient grandfather BPE vocabulary
							models (Davinci)		started the whole subword era.
		--------------|-------------------|-----------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------


Prompt building / format:
	
	🧱 Part 1: The Concept Structure (Universal Layout)

		Before looking at model-specific code, the logical arrangement of your data must always flow in a strict hierarchy. 
		You must sandwich your source chunks between your rules and the question so the model doesn't lose attention::

		[1. SYSTEM INSTRUCTIONS / CONSTRAINTS]  	<- Tell it what it can and cannot do
		[2. RETRIEVED DATA CHUNKS (THE KNOWLEDGE)] 	<- Inject your RRF winning texts
		[3. USER QUESTION]                      	<- Place the actual question at the very bottom

	🤖 Part 2: The Model-Specific Structure (Llama 3 Syntax)
		
		Because you are running Llama 3 locally inside Ollama, it expects its prompt to be wrapped inside highly specific tags 
			<|begin_of_text|>, <|start_header_id|>, etc.
		These tell the model's neural network exactly how to parse your text blocks.
	
	🔍 Why this exact layout is mandatory for Llama 3:

		• <|start_header_id|>system<|end_header_id|>: 
		  This locks down its brain. It forces it to obey your rule: "Do not make up answers."
		• <|eot_id|>: 
		  This stands for End Of Turn. It signals to the model that a specific role (like the User or System) is done talking, 
		  preventing the model from accidentally generating text as if it were the user.
		• <|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|> 
		  at the very end: This acts as a trigger phrase. It leaves the text cursor waiting right at the mouth of the model's 
		  generation layer, telling it: "Start typing your response right here."

	

	

